4.5 The 4 Mathematical Invariant Shapes

The core breakthrough of the Ledger is the principle of Topology over Tonnage.

An adversary can attempt to stay under an arbitrary numerical budget (e.g.,
keeping total risk weight < 8) by dividing actions across dozens of tiny tasks.
To neutralize this evasion, the Ledger enforces Set-Membership Invariant Checks:

\mathcal{T}_{\text{Danger}} = \{\text{EXFIL\_SHAPE}, \;\text{BEACON\_SHAPE}, \;\text{IMPLANT\_SHAPE}, \;\text{STAGING\_SHAPE}\}

\begin{aligned} \text{EXFIL_SHAPE} &\equiv {\text{ENV_READ},
;\text{ENCODE_TRANSFORM}, ;\text{NETWORK_CONNECT}} \ \text{BEACON_SHAPE} &\equiv
{\text{NETWORK_CONNECT}, ;\text{PERSISTENCE_SCHEDULE}} \ \text{IMPLANT_SHAPE}
&\equiv {\text{FILESYSTEM_WRITE}, ;\text{PROCESS_SPAWN},
;\text{PERSISTENCE_SCHEDULE}} \ \text{STAGING_SHAPE} &\equiv
{\text{FILESYSTEM_READ}, ;\text{ENCODE_TRANSFORM}, ;\text{DATA_STAGING}}
\end{aligned}

\text{Rule: If } \mathcal{S}_{\text{Current}} \supseteq \text{Shape}_i \implies \mathbf{HALT\ EXECUTION\ (Regardless\ of\ Current\ Weight)}

If an agent has accumulated the primitives to read environment data, compress
that data, and open a network socket, the system has assembled an exfiltration
capability. The Ledger halts the workflow immediately, completely bypassing
natural-language sentiment analysis.

